Below is a **complete, TypeScript-ready** example that shows how to:

1. **Create an IndexedDB store** for vector-tile blobs (`MVT` files).  
2. **Register a custom `cache://` protocol** with `maplibregl.addProtocol`.  
3. **Use the protocol in a style** (`cache://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}`).

> **TL;DR** – `addProtocol` receives a handler that must return a `Promise<ProtocolResponse>`.  
> The response can contain the raw bytes (`Uint8Array`) and optional HTTP-like headers (`cacheControl`, `expires`, `etag`, …).  
> The handler is called for every resource that starts with `cache://` – tiles, sprites, glyphs, etc. – so you can keep the logic generic.

---

## 1️⃣  Set up a tiny IndexedDB wrapper (using the tiny `idb` helper)

```ts
// db.ts --------------------------------------------------------------
import { openDB, DBSchema, IDBPDatabase } from 'idb';

interface TileDB extends DBSchema {
  /** key = `${z}/${x}/${y}` */
  tiles: {
    key: string;
    value: Uint8Array; // raw MVT bytes
  };
}

/** Singleton – open the DB once and reuse it */
let dbPromise: Promise<IDBPDatabase<TileDB>> | null = null;

export function getTileDB(): Promise<IDBPDatabase<TileDB>> {
  if (!dbPromise) {
    dbPromise = openDB<TileDB>('maplibre-tile-cache', 1, {
      upgrade(db) {
        db.createObjectStore('tiles');
      },
    });
  }
  return dbPromise;
}

/** Store a tile (you would call this when you first fetch it from the network) */
export async function putTile(
  z: number,
  x: number,
  y: number,
  data: Uint8Array,
): Promise<void> {
  const db = await getTileDB();
  const key = `${z}/${x}/${y}`;
  await db.put('tiles', data, key);
}

/** Retrieve a tile – returns undefined if not cached */
export async function getTile(
  z: number,
  x: number,
  y: number,
): Promise<Uint8Array | undefined> {
  const db = await getTileDB();
  const key = `${z}/${x}/${y}`;
  return db.get('tiles', key);
}
```

> **Why `Uint8Array`?**  
> MapLibre expects the raw binary payload (`ArrayBuffer`/`Uint8Array`). The `idb` library stores binary data without any extra conversion, so you can write the bytes you received from the network straight into the DB.

---

## 2️⃣  Register the `cache://` protocol

```ts
// protocol.ts --------------------------------------------------------
import maplibregl, { ProtocolResponse } from 'maplibre-gl';
import { getTile } from './db';

/**
 * MapLibre will call this for every URL that starts with `cache://`.
 *
 * @param params   – the parsed URL parts (`url`, `resourceType`, `zoom`, `x`, `y`, …)
 * @param abortCtrl – an AbortController that MapLibre will abort if the request is cancelled.
 *
 * @returns a Promise that resolves to a ProtocolResponse.
 */
function cacheProtocolHandler(
  params: maplibregl.ProtocolParams,
  abortCtrl: AbortController,
): Promise<ProtocolResponse> {
  // The URL we receive looks like: cache://tiles/12/3456/7890
  // Strip the scheme and leading slash:
  const path = new URL(params.url).pathname.replace(/^\/+/, ''); // "tiles/12/3456/7890"
  const parts = path.split('/');

  // We only care about the z/x/y part – ignore any prefix like "tiles"
  const z = Number(parts[parts.length - 3]);
  const x = Number(parts[parts.length - 2]);
  const y = Number(parts[parts.length - 1]);

  // If the URL is malformed, reject – MapLibre will fall back to its default error handling.
  if (Number.isNaN(z) || Number.isNaN(x) || Number.isNaN(y)) {
    return Promise.reject(new Error(`Invalid cache URL: ${params.url}`));
  }

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Try to read the tile from IndexedDB
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  const tilePromise = getTile(z, x, y);

  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Return a ProtocolResponse (or reject) – MapLibre will treat it
  //     exactly like a network response.
  // -----------------------------------------------------------------
  return tilePromise.then((bytes) => {
    if (!bytes) {
      // No cached tile → tell MapLibre to treat it as a 404.
      // You can also return a custom error response if you like.
      const err = new Error(`Tile not found in cache: ${z}/${x}/${y}`);
      // MapLibre will surface this as a “resource not found” error.
      throw err;
    }

    // Optional: add HTTP-like headers so MapLibre can cache the response
    // in its own in-memory cache (useful when you later switch to a
    // network fallback).  All fields are optional.
    const response: ProtocolResponse = {
      data: bytes, // Uint8Array or ArrayBuffer
      // cacheControl: 'max-age=86400',   // 1 day
      // expires: new Date(Date.now() + 86400 * 1000).toUTCString(),
      // etag: `"${z}-${x}-${y}"`,
    };
    return response;
  });
}

/**
 * Register the handler **once** – before you create any MapLibre map.
 */
export function registerCacheProtocol(): void {
  // `addProtocol` is promise-based since MapLibre GL JS v4.0.0.
  // The first argument is the scheme name **without** the trailing `://`.
  maplibregl.addProtocol('cache', cacheProtocolHandler);
}
```

### What the handler receives

| Property | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| `url` | Full URL string (`cache://tiles/12/3456/7890`) |
| `resourceType` | `"source"` / `"style"` / `"glyphs"` / `"sprite"` / `"tile"` … |
| `zoom`, `x`, `y` | Numeric tile coordinates **if** the URL matches the `{z}/{x}/{y}` pattern. |
| `canonicalTileURL` | The same URL but with the scheme replaced by `http://` (useful for debugging). |
| `abortController` | You can listen to `abortController.signal` to stop a long-running DB read (rare for IndexedDB). |

---

## 3️⃣  Wire everything together in your app entry point

```ts
// index.ts -----------------------------------------------------------
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
import { registerCacheProtocol } from './protocol';
import { putTile } from './db';

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Register the custom protocol **before** any MapLibreMap is created
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
registerCacheProtocol();

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  (Optional) Pre-populate the cache – this would normally be done
//     by a background fetcher that stores tiles as they are downloaded.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
async function preloadDemoTile() {
  // Example: fetch a tile from a public source and store it.
  const demoUrl = 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/tiles/planet/12/1200/1530.mvt';
  const resp = await fetch(demoUrl);
  const arrayBuffer = await resp.arrayBuffer();
  await putTile(12, 1200, 1530, new Uint8Array(arrayBuffer));
}
preloadDemoTile().catch(console.error);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Create the map – the style can now reference `cache://` URLs
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map', // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // The source points to the custom scheme.
      // Note: you can still use a normal `type: "vector"` source.
      // MapLibre will call the protocol handler for each tile request.
      cached: {
        type: 'vector',
        tiles: ['cache://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}'],
        // Optional: set a maxzoom/minzoom that matches what you stored.
        maxzoom: 14,
      },
    },
    layers: [
      {
        id: 'land',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'cached',
        'source-layer': 'land', // depends on the source's layer name
        paint: {
          'fill-color': '#e0e0e0',
        },
      },
    ],
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});
```

### What you just did

| Step | What happens under the hood |
|------|-----------------------------|
| **Register protocol** | `maplibregl.addProtocol('cache', handler)` tells the GL JS runtime “whenever you see a URL that starts with `cache://`, call *my* handler instead of doing a normal HTTP fetch”. |
| **Map creation** | When the style asks for a tile (`cache://tiles/12/1200/1530`), MapLibre parses the URL, extracts `z/x/y`, and invokes `handler(params, abortCtrl)`. |
| **Handler** | Looks up the tile in IndexedDB (`getTile`). If found, resolves with `{ data: Uint8Array }`. If not, rejects → MapLibre treats it as a 404 and will either show a transparent tile or fall back to a fallback source (if you have one). |
| **Abort support** | If the user pans/zooms fast enough that a request is no longer needed, MapLibre aborts the `AbortController`. IndexedDB reads are already fast, but you could abort a long-running fetch if you mixed network + cache. |
| **Headers (optional)** | Adding `cacheControl`, `expires`, or `etag` lets MapLibre’s internal in-memory cache work exactly like a normal HTTP response. Useful if you later switch to a hybrid “network-first, cache-fallback” strategy. |

---

## 4️⃣  Bonus: Hybrid “Network-first, Cache-fallback” handler

If you want the app to **try the network first** and fall back to the IndexedDB cache when offline, just wrap the network fetch in the same handler:

```ts
function hybridCacheHandler(
  params: maplibregl.ProtocolParams,
  abortCtrl: AbortController,
): Promise<ProtocolResponse> {
  const url = new URL(params.url);
  const path = url.pathname.replace(/^\/+/, '');
  const parts = path.split('/');
  const z = Number(parts[parts.length - 3]);
  const x = Number(parts[parts.length - 2]);
  const y = Number(parts[parts.length - 1]);

  // Build the *real* HTTP URL that the tiles originally live at.
  // In this example we assume the same pattern under https://mytiles.com/
  const httpUrl = `https://mytiles.com/tiles/${z}/${x}/${y}.mvt`;

  // 1️⃣ Try network (respect abort)
  const networkPromise = fetch(httpUrl, { signal: abortCtrl.signal })
    .then(async (resp) => {
      if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`Network error ${resp.status}`);
      const buf = await resp.arrayBuffer();
      const bytes = new Uint8Array(buf);
      // Store a copy in IndexedDB for next time
      await putTile(z, x, y, bytes);
      return {
        data: bytes,
        cacheControl: resp.headers.get('Cache-Control') ?? undefined,
        expires: resp.headers.get('Expires') ?? undefined,
        etag: resp.headers.get('ETag') ?? undefined,
      } as ProtocolResponse;
    })
    .catch(() => null); // swallow network errors – we’ll try the cache

  // 2️⃣ If network failed, read from cache
  const cachePromise = networkPromise.then((result) => {
    if (result) return result; // network succeeded
    return getTile(z, x, y).then((bytes) => {
      if (!bytes) throw new Error('Tile not in cache');
      return { data: bytes } as ProtocolResponse;
    });
  });

  return cachePromise;
}

// Register under the same scheme (or a new one)
maplibregl.addProtocol('cache', hybridCacheHandler);
```

Now the same `cache://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}` URL works **online** (fast network) **and** **offline** (fallback to IndexedDB).

---

