## 1️⃣  Set up a tiny IndexedDB wrapper (using the tiny `idb-keyval` helper)

You can roll your own IndexedDB code, but the **`idb-keyval`** package gives a clean, promise-based API that works in all modern browsers.

```bash
# npm install idb-keyval
npm i idb-keyval
```

```ts
// src/cache.ts
import { get, set, del, createStore, Store } from 'idb-keyval';

/**
 * The IndexedDB store that holds raw MVT bytes.
 * The key is the tile URL *without* the scheme, e.g. "tiles/12/3456/7890".
 */
export const tileStore: Store = createStore('mbtiles-cache', 'tiles');

/**
 * Store a tile.
 * @param z zoom level
 * @param x tile column
 * @param y tile row
 * @param data raw MVT bytes (ArrayBuffer or Uint8Array)
 */
export async function putTile(
  z: number,
  x: number,
  y: number,
  data: ArrayBuffer | Uint8Array
): Promise<void> {
  const key = `tiles/${z}/${x}/${y}`;
  await set(key, data, tileStore);
}

/**
 * Retrieve a tile – returns `undefined` if it is not cached.
 */
export async function getTile(
  z: number,
  x: number,
  y: number
): Promise<ArrayBuffer | undefined> {
  const key = `tiles/${z}/${x}/${y}`;
  const value = await get<ArrayBuffer>(key, tileStore);
  return value;
}

/**
 * Remove a tile (optional utility).
 */
export async function deleteTile(z: number, x: number, y: number): Promise<void> {
  const key = `tiles/${z}/${x}/${y}`;
  await del(key, tileStore);
}
```

> **Why the key is `tiles/...` and not `cache://…`?**  
> `addProtocol` hands you the *full* URL (`cache://tiles/12/3456/7890`). The handler strips the scheme and uses the remainder as the DB key – this keeps the DB tidy and lets you reuse the same store for other schemes if you wish.

---

## 2️⃣  Register the `cache://` protocol

```ts
// src/protocol.ts
import maplibregl, { Resource, ResourceResponse } from 'maplibre-gl';
import { getTile } from './cache';

/**
 * The protocol handler.
 *
 * @param params   The parsed URL parts (see MapLibre docs)
 * @param abortCtrl  An AbortController that MapLibre will signal if the request is cancelled.
 *
 * @returns a Promise that resolves to a ResourceResponse.
 */
function cacheProtocolHandler(
  params: Resource,
  abortCtrl: AbortController
): Promise<ResourceResponse> {
  // `params.url` looks like "cache://tiles/12/3456/7890"
  const url = new URL(params.url);
  const [, zStr, xStr, yStr] = url.pathname.split('/'); // ["", "tiles", "12", "3456", "7890"]
  const z = Number(zStr);
  const x = Number(xStr);
  const y = Number(yStr);

  // If the request is aborted (e.g. user pans away), stop the DB lookup.
  if (abortCtrl.signal.aborted) {
    return Promise.reject(new Error('request aborted'));
  }

  // Try to read the tile from IndexedDB.
  return getTile(z, x, y).then((blob) => {
    if (!blob) {
      // No cached tile → reject so MapLibre falls back to the normal HTTP fetch.
      // The rejection message is ignored; just throw.
      throw new Error('tile not in cache');
    }

    // The response shape MapLibre expects:
    //   data: ArrayBuffer | string | Blob
    //   cacheControl?: string
    //   expires?: string
    //   etag?: string
    //   modified?: string
    //   expires?: string
    //   // …any other HTTP-like headers you want to expose
    const response: ResourceResponse = {
      data: blob, // already an ArrayBuffer (or Uint8Array)
      // optional: you can add caching headers if you like
      // cacheControl: 'max-age=31536000',
    };
    return response;
  });
}

/**
 * Register the protocol **once** (usually at app start, before creating the map).
 */
export function registerCacheProtocol(): void {
  // The first argument is the scheme *without* the trailing colon.
  maplibregl.addProtocol('cache', cacheProtocolHandler);
}
```

### What the handler does

| Step | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **Parse the URL** – `new URL(params.url)` | Gives you `pathname`, `search`, etc. |
| **Extract `z/x/y`** | Those are the keys you stored in IndexedDB. |
| **Abort handling** – check `abortCtrl.signal.aborted` | Prevents unnecessary DB work when the user quickly pans/zooms. |
| **`getTile`** – read from IndexedDB | Returns `ArrayBuffer | undefined`. |
| **If missing → reject** | MapLibre will automatically try the normal network request (useful for a hybrid cache-first strategy). |
| **If found → resolve** with a `ResourceResponse` containing the raw bytes | MapLibre will treat the bytes as an MVT tile and render it. |

> **Important:** `addProtocol` must be called **before** any `Map` instance is created, otherwise the map will have already resolved the style and fetched tiles using the default HTTP stack.

---

## 3️⃣  Put it all together – a minimal app

```tsx
// src/index.tsx
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom/client';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
import { registerCacheProtocol } from './protocol';
import { putTile } from './cache';

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Register the custom protocol (once, at startup)
registerCacheProtocol();

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  (Optional) Pre-populate the cache for demo purposes.
//    In a real app you would fill the cache while the user browses,
//    or during an offline-download step.
async function seedCache() {
  // Example: fetch a single tile from the network and store it.
  const z = 12,
    x = 3456,
    y = 7890;
  const remoteUrl = `https://example.com/tiles/${z}/${x}/${y}.pbf`;

  const resp = await fetch(remoteUrl);
  if (!resp.ok) throw new Error('failed to fetch demo tile');
  const arrayBuffer = await resp.arrayBuffer();

  await putTile(z, x, y, arrayBuffer);
  console.log('demo tile cached');
}
seedCache().catch(console.error);

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Create the map – note the style uses the custom scheme.
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map', // <div id="map"></div> in index.html
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // The source points at the custom scheme.
      // MapLibre will call our handler for every tile request.
      osm: {
        type: 'vector',
        tiles: ['cache://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}'],
        // Optional: a fallback URL for when a tile is not cached.
        // MapLibre will try this *after* the protocol handler rejects.
        // (You can also omit it and let the handler forward to the network.)
        // fallback: ['https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf']
      },
    },
    layers: [
      {
        id: 'roads',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'osm',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#ff6600',
          'line-width': 2,
        },
      },
    ],
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});

map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());
```

```html
<!-- public/index.html -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – cache:// protocol demo</title>
  <style>
    body, html, #map { margin:0; padding:0; width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div id="map"></div>
  <script type="module" src="/src/index.tsx"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What you’ll see

* If the tile you seeded (`z=12, x=3456, y=7890`) falls inside the current viewport, it will be rendered **directly from IndexedDB** (no network request in the dev-tools “Network” tab).  
* For any other tile, the handler rejects → MapLibre falls back to the normal HTTP request (or you can provide a `fallback` array in the source definition).

